-- Seed 0004 — the production academic catalog (audit C1).
--
-- ## Why this file exists
--
-- Seed 0002 contains 5 colleges, 10 careers and 16 programs. It is a fixture: enough to prove the
-- engine runs, and *actively misleading* as a demonstration of what the engine does. §27 ranks the
-- catalog and keeps the top ten (`TOP_N` in src/lib/recommendation.ts), so with exactly ten careers
-- in the database every student receives **every career in the system**, reordered. Two students
-- with opposite RIASEC profiles get identical lists. The ranking is real and the arithmetic is
-- correct; there is simply nothing for it to discriminate between.
--
-- This file is the catalog that makes the ranking mean something: 20 institutions, 68 careers,
-- 48 canonical programs and 300+ offerings, so the top ten is a genuine selection out of many and
-- two different students visibly receive two different answers.
--
-- ## Where the data comes from
--
-- **Programs** are CHED priority degree programmes, using CHED's own programme names.
-- **Careers** sit inside the PSOC 2012 major groups (chiefly Professionals and Technicians and
-- Associate Professionals). **Institutions** are real Philippine HEIs, and the programmes attached
-- to each are drawn from what that institution actually offers.
--
-- Salary bands and `typical_riasec_code` are **considered estimates, not measurements**, and should
-- be read that way: monthly PHP ranges for the Philippine market, and Holland codes assigned from
-- the standard occupational interpretation of each role. They are seed values an administrator is
-- expected to refine — every one of them is editable in the admin catalog screens. Nothing here is
-- presented to a student as a citation.
--
-- ## Shape notes
--
-- `typical_riasec_code` is read **positionally** by §27, weighted [0.5, 0.3, 0.2] — the letter order
-- is data, not formatting.
--
-- `recommended_strand` NULL is a *claim* — "this programme has no strand requirement" — which §27
-- scores as a full 100. It is not "unknown". BFA, AB Communication and AB Political Science are the
-- rows that carry it.
--
-- `program_catalog` rows are written **here** rather than left to migration 0018's backfill, which
-- ran once against the programs that existed at the time. Without them every programme seeded below
-- would have `program_catalog_id = NULL`, and the student-facing "which colleges offer this
-- programme?" screen would correctly answer "this programme has not been matched to the shared
-- programme catalog yet" for the entire catalog.
--
-- Idempotent **with respect to itself**: every row is keyed by a fixed UUIDv5 derived from its
-- natural key and inserted with `INSERT OR IGNORE`, so re-running this file changes nothing and
-- never duplicates.
--
-- ⚠️  **Do not run this together with seed 0002.** The two overlap on five colleges and ten careers
-- by *name* — "University of the Philippines Diliman", "Software Engineer", "Teacher" and so on —
-- and they carry different ids, because 0002's are hand-written and these are derived. Neither
-- `colleges.name` nor `careers.title` has a unique index, so `INSERT OR IGNORE` has nothing to
-- collide on and both rows survive. A database with both seeds holds 25 colleges and 78 careers,
-- ten of them duplicates.
--
-- That is not a cosmetic problem: §27 ranks every active career, so two identically-coded
-- "Software Engineer" rows score identically and **both appear in the same student's top ten** —
-- the duplicate card is visible on the recommendations screen. `npm run db:seed` therefore chains
-- 0001 → **0004** → 0003 and skips 0002 entirely; `db:seed:catalog` still exists to run 0002 alone
-- for anyone reproducing pre-audit behaviour.
--
-- The remaining asymmetry, if you have already run both: 0002's sixteen programs carry
-- `program_catalog_id = NULL` (it predates migration 0018, whose backfill runs at migration time
-- and so sees no rows in a fresh database), which makes them invisible to the student-facing
-- "which colleges offer this programme?" screen. The fix is a clean reseed, not a patch.
--
-- Timestamps are ISO-8601 UTC (`strftime`), never SQLite's bare `CURRENT_TIMESTAMP`, which renders
-- as `2026-07-13 14:11:05` and which JavaScript reads as *local* time (src/lib/datetime.ts).

